@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
' purpose: the docker-side credential broker — what it attaches, and what it removes
' covers: internal/broker/broker.go
'
' ── THE POINT IS THE STRIP, NOT THE INJECT ─────────────────────────────────
' The broker exists so a provider key never enters the container on the enforced
' egress tiers. The container gets a SENTINEL; the real value lives in the egress
' layer and is attached at the proxy, on route, per provider.
'
' The half that is easy to forget is the other one: on an OFF-ROUTE request the
' broker STRIPS credential headers. Injection alone would be a half-measure — an
' agent that has learned a key from anywhere else could still post it to a host
' the operator never approved, and the enforced tier would have bought nothing.
' DefaultStripHeaders is that floor, applied whenever the broker is active and no
' explicit strip list is configured.
'
' ── ROUTE IS DECIDED BY HOST, NOT BY TRUST ─────────────────────────────────
' IsProviderHost matches the request's host — including the port and proxy-request
' shapes, which is where this kind of check usually leaks. A request is on-route
' because of where it is GOING, never because of who sent it.
'
' Some providers authenticate by query parameter rather than header, so the broker
' injects there too; a header-only broker would silently fail to protect exactly
' the providers whose secret is most exposed in a URL.
'
' ── THE SBX BACKEND DOES NOT USE ANY OF THIS ───────────────────────────────
' Worth stating plainly, because "broker" appears on both backends and means
' different machinery. There is no proveo egress layer inside a sandbox: sbx holds
' the secret in its own store and its proxy injects. Same guarantee, different
' party — see _spec/internal/sbx/credential-path.puml.
title internal/broker — inject on route, strip off it

participant "agent\n(holds a sentinel)" as AG <<app>>
participant "broker\nin the egress layer" as BR <<cloud>>
participant "provider host" as PR <<cloud>>
participant "anywhere else" as X <<error>>

AG ARROW_MAIN BR : request + sentinel
BR ARROW_MAIN PR : on route — real value attached\n(header or query param)
BR ARROW_ERROR X : off route — credential headers STRIPPED

note over BR
  **Stripping is not the optional half.** Without
  it a key learned from anywhere else still leaves
  the box, and the enforced tier means nothing.
end note
@enduml
